\@writefile{lof}{\defcounter {refsection}{0}\relax }\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Comparing the least squares solution (top) to a solution using logarithmically transformed data (bottom). Both figures share a common scale. The black line represents the data vector $R$ and is the same for both cases; the blue line represents the range space component $\alpha _{p} {\color  {blue}{ t^{\frac  {2}{5}} }} $. The short leg of each figure is the residual error. On top, this vector is orthogonal to the range space solution and as a null space vector it is colored red. Once the data is transformed with the logarithm it is no longer possible to find the orthogonal decomposition guaranteed by the Fundamental Theorem.}}{27}{figure.5.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:compare-triangles-tikz}{{5.2}{27}{Comparing the least squares solution (top) to a solution using logarithmically transformed data (bottom). Both figures share a common scale. The black line represents the data vector $R$ and is the same for both cases; the blue line represents the range space component $\alpha _{p}\bttf $. The short leg of each figure is the residual error. On top, this vector is orthogonal to the range space solution and as a null space vector it is colored red. Once the data is transformed with the logarithm it is no longer possible to find the orthogonal decomposition guaranteed by the Fundamental Theorem}{figure.5.2}{}}
